  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "If \\(x=\\frac{a}{b},\\) \\(a\\ne b,\\) and \\(b\\ne0,\\) then \\(\\dfrac{a+b}{a-b}=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac{x}{x+1}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac{x+1}{x-1}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(x-\\frac1x\\)",
    "E": "\\(x+\\frac1x\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Since \\(x=a/b\\), divide both the numerator and denominator by \\(b\\) to make that ratio appear. This is valid because \\(b\\ne0\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a+b}{a-b}\n=\\frac{a/b+1}{a/b-1}\n=\\frac{x+1}{x-1}.\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(x-1=(a-b)/b\\ne0\\), so the resulting fraction is defined under the original assumptions. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{x+1}{x-1}}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
